WEBVTT

s1.1
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.704
Symphony runs coding agents on tickets from Linear or GitHub Issues.

s1.2
00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:07.309
Each ticket gets an agent in its own workspace.

s1.3
00:00:07.309 --> 00:00:11.221
The agent opens a pull request. A merger reviews and merges it.

s1.4
00:00:11.221 --> 00:00:13.420
People stay in control from Tern.

s2.1
00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:17.621
Webhooks from the tracker and from GitHub reach the control API.

s2.2
00:00:17.621 --> 00:00:21.043
The API wakes the daemon. The daemon starts a runner Job.

s2.3
00:00:21.043 --> 00:00:23.132
The runner starts omp in a workspace pod.

s2.4
00:00:23.132 --> 00:00:26.670
The pod can reach the gateway, but not the API or Redis.

s2.5
00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.453
Redis holds all shared state.

s2.6
00:00:28.453 --> 00:00:32.409
The merger merges pull requests. An assistant bot answers in chat.

s2.7
00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:36.105
Run it on Kubernetes. One VM with k3s is enough.

s2.8
00:00:36.105 --> 00:00:40.598
Local mode runs the API, the daemon and the runners in one process.

s3.1
00:00:40.948 --> 00:00:45.838
A ticket needs the symphony label. On GitHub, the board's Status column is its state.

s3.2
00:00:45.838 --> 00:00:48.849
The agent moves it to In Progress, then to In Review.

s3.3
00:00:48.849 --> 00:00:51.353
The merger sends it to Rework, or to Merging.

s3.4
00:00:51.353 --> 00:00:53.554
After the merge, the ticket goes to Done.

s3.5
00:00:53.554 --> 00:00:56.051
At the turn limit, the same attempt continues.

s3.6
00:00:56.051 --> 00:00:58.443
A failed attempt retries after a backoff.

s3.7
00:00:58.443 --> 00:01:00.887
A person can hold the ticket at any time.

s4.1
00:01:01.237 --> 00:01:04.140
A tracker webhook wakes the daemon in under 3 seconds.

s4.2
00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.626
The daemon takes a dispatch token. Only that dispatch can write.

s4.3
00:01:07.626 --> 00:01:10.217
The runner makes or reuses the workspace pod.

s4.4
00:01:10.217 --> 00:01:13.534
It gets a GitHub token for the ticket's repositories only.

s4.5
00:01:13.534 --> 00:01:15.836
omp works in turns and streams each event.

s4.6
00:01:15.836 --> 00:01:19.717
It opens a ready pull request and moves the ticket to In Review.

s5.1
00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:22.376
Tern shows the live sessions on the dashboard.

s5.2
00:01:22.376 --> 00:01:24.215
Open any session as a live chat.

s5.3
00:01:24.215 --> 00:01:28.447
Messages and interrupts reach the agent in under 300 milliseconds.

s5.4
00:01:28.447 --> 00:01:31.924
The agent can ask you a question. It never waits without a limit.

s5.5
00:01:31.924 --> 00:01:34.783
Take over a ticket to work on it with omp on your machine.

s5.6
00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:36.737
Return it, and Symphony continues.

s5.7
00:01:36.737 --> 00:01:40.576
In Slack or Discord, the assistant answers with cited code lines.

s5.8
00:01:40.576 --> 00:01:44.315
Its plans become tickets only after a mapped person confirms.

s5.9
00:01:44.315 --> 00:01:47.497
The CLI and MCP give the same controls.

s6.1
00:01:47.847 --> 00:01:51.622
One merger serves every repository, and each has its own train.

s6.2
00:01:51.622 --> 00:01:54.402
Reviewers check the pull request in parallel lanes.

s6.3
00:01:54.402 --> 00:01:57.199
An oracle on a different model checks each finding.

s6.4
00:01:57.199 --> 00:01:59.777
A finding blocks only when both models agree.

s6.5
00:01:59.777 --> 00:02:03.930
A pull request goes back one time. After that, an arbiter decides.

s6.6
00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.412
A re-review reads only the new changes.

s6.7
00:02:06.412 --> 00:02:08.789
If CI still runs, the review waits for it.

s6.8
00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:11.834
The train chains a batch and runs CI on the top only.

s6.9
00:02:11.834 --> 00:02:15.407
A red batch splits in halves until the bad pull request is alone.

s6.10
00:02:15.407 --> 00:02:20.079
Agents resolve conflicts. The train merges the exact head that CI tested.

s6.11
00:02:20.079 --> 00:02:23.102
When main turns red, the merger opens a ticket.

s7.1
00:02:23.452 --> 00:02:26.988
The team configuration gives each agent role its own model.

s7.2
00:02:26.988 --> 00:02:31.865
Admins save provider API keys in Tern. The API never returns them.

s7.3
00:02:31.865 --> 00:02:36.499
MCP servers give a role more tools. Their secret headers stay in the gateway.

s7.4
00:02:36.499 --> 00:02:44.196
A workspace gets only its role's model key, a short-lived GitHub token, a telemetry token and an MCP session token.

s7.5
00:02:44.196 --> 00:02:49.839
The Linear key, the App key, Slack or Discord tokens, and API tokens stay outside.

s8.1
00:02:50.189 --> 00:02:53.160
Each vendor mock follows the published specification.

s8.2
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:58.692
With Linear and Slack, real agents passed all 8 scenarios in 278 seconds.

s8.3
00:02:58.692 --> 00:03:03.278
With GitHub and Discord, 4 scenarios passed in 183 seconds.

s8.4
00:03:03.278 --> 00:03:07.800
The assistant passed on Slack, on Discord, and with MCP through the gateway.

s8.5
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.521
A webhook started a session 44 milliseconds after the ticket.

s9.1
00:03:11.871 --> 00:03:13.838
Install the API and Redis.

s9.2
00:03:13.838 --> 00:03:16.697
Then run symphony setup, or open Setup in Tern.

s9.3
00:03:16.697 --> 00:03:18.650
Each step is checked before it is saved.

s9.4
00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:20.518
The docs have every step.
